-- title:
--   Theorem 5.1 — Uniform Hellinger control for near-MLEs
-- statement:
--   Fix $d\ge1$, $S\ge0$, and a nonempty compact $K\subset\mathbb R^d$ with $\|\theta\|\le S$ on $K$. For every $b>0$, there exist $C>0$ and $n_0$ such that for every $n\ge n_0$ and every true law $G^*\in\mathcal P(K)$, iid observations from the unit-covariance Gaussian mixture $f_{G^*}$ admit a measurable event $E$ with $\mathbb P(E^c)\le Cn^{-b}$. For every $x\in E$ and every candidate $G\in\mathcal P(K)$, $\sum_i\log f_G(x_i)\ge\sum_i\log f_{G^*}(x_i)-1$ implies $H^2(f_G,f_{G^*})\le C(\log n)^{d+1}/[n(\log\log n)^d]$. The event is chosen before the candidate law, the likelihood tolerance is one unit of total log-likelihood, and Hellinger loss is integrated over all of $\mathbb R^d$ without a factor $1/2$.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/hanshengjiang/reweighted-npmle-lean/blob/5af8d16822c5a8a3f032b13eb95daea40f307484/ReweightedNPMLE/GaussianNearMLETheorem.lean#L15-L56

